-- statement:
--   For nonunital complex C*-algebras A and B, a spatial representation consists of complex Hilbert spaces H and K and nonunital *-homomorphisms A→B(H) and B→B(K). The spatial ambient algebra is the algebra of uniformly bounded operator families, one acting on the completed Hilbert tensor product H⊗K for each such representation. The elementary tensor associated with a∈A and b∈B is the family of completed tensor-product operators; its norm is bounded by ‖a‖‖b‖. Supporting lemmas show that extending a continuous complex-linear operator to completions preserves its operator norm. MinimalTensor(A,B) is the closed nonunital *-subalgebra generated by all elementary tensors, and is itself a nonunital C*-algebra. For a *-homomorphism q:C→D, tensorKernelIdeal(q) is the closed *-subalgebra generated by elementary tensors a⊗c with q(c)=0. Exact(A) is the defined proposition that, for every surjective q and every z∈MinimalTensor(A,C), if z evaluates to zero at every spatial representation obtained from a representation of A and D by composing its right-hand representation with q, then z belongs to tensorKernelIdeal(q). For finite square matrices a and b over an ordered nonunital C*-algebra, Cuntz subequivalence a≼b means that for every ε>0 there is a rectangular matrix v with ‖v* b v−a‖<ε; this definition itself imposes no positivity condition. Block sum is the diagonal block matrix a⊕b, proper infiniteness of a means a⊕a≼a, and diagonalCopies(n,a) is the n-by-n diagonal matrix with every diagonal entry a. Weak pure infiniteness means that one integer n≥1 makes diagonalCopies(n,a) properly infinite for every nonzero positive a. Strong pure infiniteness means that for every positive a,b and ε>0 there exist s,t∈A satisfying ‖s*a²s−a²‖<ε, ‖t*b²t−b²‖<ε, and ‖s*abt‖<ε.
